  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "F9rmNQQ3MTgMOrS8u67qGt1aPEA=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:38:23.893Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The academy marks this as an explicit anti-pattern warning, not a style preference. `reactorClient.execute()` does not return until its job reaches `READ_READY`, and `READ_READY` is only reached after the post-ready phase \u2014 which is where processors run. A processor that calls `reactorClient.execute()` can therefore end up waiting on the processor pipeline it is currently occupying.\n\n```typescript\n// Bad: blocks until the job completes\nconst doc = await reactorClient.execute(docId, \"main\", actions);\n\n// Good: submits and returns immediately\nawait this.dispatch.execute(docId, \"main\", actions);\n```\n\nThree reasons are given, in order of severity:\n\n1. **Potential blocking.** The call could wait on processors finishing their current batch, including the caller.\n2. **Unnecessary round-trip.** The resulting operations arrive through `onOperations` anyway, so awaiting them synchronously buys latency and nothing else.\n3. **One-way flow.** React to operations, dispatch new work, move on. If you need the result, handle it in a later `onOperations` call.\n\nThere is a trap in the type surface here. Connect's host module is `IReactorProcessorHostModule`, which *extends* `IProcessorHostModule` with `client: IReactorClient` and `attachments: IAttachmentClient` \u2014 so a full `IReactorClient` genuinely is reachable from a browser-hosted processor. It is there for reads and for hosts that need it, and reaching for its `execute()` compiles cleanly. The guardrail is the convention, not the type system.\n\nProvenance: academy `03-Build/04-WorkWithData/05-ProcessorBestPractices.md` (published, \"Why not reactorClient?\" warning callout), cross-checked against `packages/reactor-browser/src/types/processor-host-module.ts` at 6.2.2-dev.45.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T14:38:22.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:38:23.893Z"
    }
  },
